# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-17106 (GHSA-hfg8-hc9c-6c3h) — moby/go-archive: crafted tar
# archive can write outside the extraction directory ("CopyEscape")
#
# Found in: usr/libexec/docker/cli-plugins/docker-buildx
# Fixed in: golang.org/moby/go-archive 0.3.0 (installed: v0.2.1)
#
# DIFFERENT RISK CATEGORY from every other entry in this file — flagged
# here explicitly rather than treated as routine. This is not a low-
# severity or theoretical finding: CopyEscape is a real, actively-
# discussed CVSS 7.1 container-to-host arbitrary file write via crafted
# tar extraction (a TOCTOU race resolving absolute symlinks during
# extraction), covered in independent security research under its own
# name, with Docker treating it as serious enough for a coordinated
# disclosure extension and a follow-up hardening release after the first
# fix caused functional regressions. Given a Docker security team invested
# that much care in getting the fix right, "buildx hasn't rebuilt yet"
# deserves real scrutiny before being treated the same as an ordinary
# dependency lag.
#
# Confirmed, not assumed, that no fix is currently installable: as of
# 2026-08-27, v0.36.1 is docker/buildx's actual latest release (GitHub's
# own release list, tagged [Latest], released 2026-08-04) — and its
# complete "Dependency Changes" section lists exactly one bump,
# `github.com/moby/buildkit v0.32.0 -> v0.32.2`. No go-archive change at
# all since v0.36.0 (which itself only reached v0.2.1, confirmed via that
# release's own dependency diff). golang.org/moby/go-archive has iterated
# quickly since the July 30 fix (0.3.0 -> 0.3.1 -> 0.3.2 -> 0.3.3, the
# last three addressing real regressions and correctness issues from the
# first hardening pass) — buildx has adopted none of them yet. Contrast
# with docker-compose (clean in this same scan): compose carries its own
# *direct* go-archive dependency, independent of whatever buildx vendors,
# and has already bumped it straight through to 0.3.3 — this is why the
# two plugins diverge here despite shipping side by side in this image.
#
# NOT CONFIRMED: whether buildx's own extraction code paths (build
# context handling, cache import/export) actually reach the vulnerable
# symbols in an attacker-reachable way comparable to the `docker cp`
# vector the CVE was disclosed against. Left in rather than dropped for
# the same reason as the yq/golang.org-net entry above — Trivy's scan is
# version-based, not reachability-based, so the finding stands regardless
# — but this caveat is recorded so nobody reads this entry as "confirmed
# safe," only as "confirmed unfixable this week."
#
# SHORT EXPIRY, DELIBERATELY NOT THE STANDING 2026-11-10 CHECKPOINT:
# given the pace of go-archive's own point releases and the level of
# attention this CVE has received, a fix landing in buildx within days is
# plausible, not a months-out prospect. Re-check docker/buildx's release
# list directly before 2026-09-05 — if v0.36.1 is no longer the latest
# tag and the new release's dependency diff shows go-archive at 0.3.0 or
# above, drop this entry; if not, re-verify and extend deliberately rather
# than let this lapse silently into a routine, unreviewed suppression.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CVE-2026-17106 exp:2026-09-05

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 — msgpack: Out-of-bounds read / crash on Unpacker reuse
#
# Found in: pip's own vendored copy (pip/_vendor/msgpack), NOT a separately
# pip-installed package — reported by Trivy under a path-less "Python"
# target rather than a discoverable dist-info, because vendored source has
# no independent package metadata for pip's own dependency resolver to
# manage. `pip install --upgrade msgpack` anywhere (system, pipx shared,
# Poetry's own venv) does not reach this; those three install sites are
# confirmed already at msgpack 1.2.1 in this build.
#
# Fixed in: msgpack 1.2.1 (released 2026-06-19). Live-checked 2026-08-17
# against pip's own vendor.txt across the latest available release
# (26.2.1 — confirmed via `git ls-remote --tags` against pypa/pip, no
# newer tag exists): still pins msgpack==1.1.2. Same category as the
# CVE-2025-47273 setuptools entry above — a dependency frozen inside a
# third-party redistributed artifact, unfixable from our side until pip
# ships a release with an updated vendor.txt.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-11-10
